On Sat, 17 Oct 2026 00:04:55 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> Do some more research. There's a lot more information on their workflow,
> threading model, Junio's workflow, etc than just that.

You were right, and my last reply was wrong. Until June 2026,
git.git's rule was yours: the cover of v(N+1) replies to the cover of
vN, so the versions form a spine. Junio still stated that preference
on 4 June. git.git then switched to replying to v1, and the page I
quoted is the result. A commit dated 15 June, merged in July,
rewrote MyFirstContribution from "previous iteration" to "first
iteration". GitGitGadget followed on 4 August, and practice on the
list has moved with it.

So "as git.git does" in patch 1 is true only of the last four months.
It is not the long-standing rule, and in my last reply I was wrong to
call your spine "b4's yes, not git.git's rule".

Evidence follows: what Junio said, the change itself, what the tools
do, and what a year of the list shows.

What Junio said
---------------

On 4 June, in "Re: [PATCH 1/2] b4: introduce configuration for the
Git project" [1]:

  "We do not have a word to describe how the latest iteration refers
  to its previous iteration via In-reply-to: or References: headers,
  but our preference is to make the cover letter of iteration N+1 to
  be a response to the cover letter of iteration N."

  "Having said that, I've seen a cover letter of iteration N (for any
  value of N > 1) that respondes to the cover letter of the initial
  iteration. While it seems not to break "br" and the lore archive
  does not seem unhappy about it, I am not sure if tooling used by
  other people are also happy with it."

He wrote the same thing a month earlier, in "Re: [PATCH v4 0/3] Avoid
hardcoded "good"/"bad" bisect terms" [2]. There he asked that "[v4
0/3] must be a reply to [v3 0/2]. Otherwise b4 will say that v3 is
still the last iteration".

In both messages his concern is tooling, not shape. He replaces a
topic by handing "b4 am" the Message-ID of a patch he has queued; b4
then follows the thread to the newest round, and he compares the two
with "git range-diff @{-1}...". He describes this in "Re: [PATCH v2]
doc: document and test `@` prefix for raw timestamps" [3] and in "Re:
[PATCH v2] ls-files: filter pathspec before lstat" [4]. He also says
there that a "Link to vN" line is not a substitute for In-Reply-To.
A new thread for each version breaks his workflow, and the list
regulars all reject it. Which earlier cover you reply to matters
much less to him, and in "Re: [PATCH v3 0/3] Documentation: recommend
the use of b4" [5] he gave way:

  "As long as it does not hurt automation, I do not care too much"

The change
----------

Patrick Steinhardt's series "[PATCH v4 0/3] Documentation: recommend
the use of b4" [6] made the switch. Its commit 16b2924aa,
"MyFirstContribution: recommend shallow threading of cover letters",
says:

  "The "MyFirstContribution" document recommends the use of deep
  threading of cover letters: every cover letter of subsequent
  iterations shall be linked to the cover letter of the preceding
  version. The result of this is that eventually, threads with many
  versions are getting nested so deep that it becomes hard to
  follow."

The same series added .b4-config with "send-same-thread = shallow".
In "Re: [PATCH v2 1/3] Documentation/MyFirstContribution: recommend
shallow threading", Kristoffer Haugsbakk gave the practical reason:
on lore in mobile Safari, deep threads become "unclickable backticks"
[7]. In the same thread Weijie Yuan argued for the spine. Each cover
carries a range-diff against the previous version, so "chain-reply-to
makes more sence to me" [8]. Toon Claes, on v3 of the series, did not
"think it's worth it to keep bike shedding about this" [12]. In the
v4 cover letter Patrick wrote "I wasn't really able to judge
consensus one way or the other" [6]. Junio merged it, and "What's
cooking in git.git (Jul 2026, #03)" lists ps/doc-recommend-b4 as
graduated to master [9].

After the change Junio spelled out the new shape himself, in "Re:
[PATCH v2 0/5] Introduce 'uploadpack.lazyFetchTrusted'" [10]:

  "* vN (1 < N) cover letter is a reply to v1 cover letter
   * vN patch M (0 < M) is a reply to vN cover letter"

Two points stay unsettled. SubmittingPatches never names v1 or
v(N-1); it says only to reply to "either an additional 'cover
letter' message (see below), the first patch, or the respective
preceding patch", and the series left that unchanged. And Junio
said on 4 June that a single-patch topic chains each version to the
one before it [1]; no document has addressed that since.

The tools
---------

- b4: src/b4/ez.py sets "oldrev = revision - 1" and then, for
  shallow, "oldrev = 1". So "yes" replies to the previous cover and
  "shallow" replies to the first, as b4's config docs say. b4's own
  releases.rst says the opposite about shallow; that is a b4 doc bug.
  b4 defaults to "no", which starts a new thread, but git.git's
  .b4-config overrides that default.
- GitGitGadget: until 4 August, lib/patch-series.ts passed every
  earlier cover to format-patch as --in-reply-to. Only the last one
  took effect, so each version replied to the one before it. Commit
  567b4d8, "feat: use shallow threading for new versions of patches"
  (merged as cadedf4, PR #2309), now passes only the first. The merge
  message cites git.git's 16b2924aa, and the commit closes
  gitgitgadget issue #2254, which Derrick Stolee opened on 3 June.
- send-email and format-patch: --thread=shallow and deep concern only
  the mails within one version, as SZEDER Gábor pointed out in
  "Re: [PATCH 1/2] b4: introduce configuration for the Git project"
  [11]. The --in-reply-to example in git-send-email(1) has only two
  versions, so it cannot tell v1 apart from v(N-1).

