Current practice
----------------

lore's web pages are behind a bot wall, but its git inbox can be
cloned. I took a year of the list (October 2025 on, 27,630 messages).
For each v3 or later series I followed the In-Reply-To of its cover,
or of its only patch. I left out v2, where both rules give the same
answer. Before 5 August 2026, 459 replied to v(N-1) and 144 to v1.
From 5 August on, 52 replied to v(N-1) and 157 to v1.

By sender:

- GitGitGadget: 253 to v(N-1) up to 4 August, then 87 to v1 and none
  to v(N-1). The switch is clean, and it lands on the day the commit
  above was merged.
- b4: mostly v1 (115 against 38), from git.git's .b4-config.
  Patrick Steinhardt replied to v1 71 times out of 71, and Karthik
  Nayak 32 out of 32.
- Plain send-email still leans toward v(N-1), 47 against 35 since
  August, because contributors set --in-reply-to by hand.
- Some regulars keep the spine. Justin Tobler chained 16 of 16, and
  Toon Claes, who uses b4, 17 of 18. Junio's own series are split,
  6 to v(N-1) and 7 to v1.

Across v2 and later, another 116 series heads replied to a review
message rather than a cover, and 93 started a new thread.

Recent examples of each shape:

- Spine: "[PATCH v24 0/7] branch: delete-merged" replies to its v23
  cover [13]. Its v25 came after GitGitGadget's switch and replies to
  v1, so one thread now holds both shapes.
- Fan: "[PATCH v9 0/5] fetch: avoid fetching every branch of a new
  remote in a shallow repo" replies to its v1 patch [14].

> Reply with conclusions and evidence.

Conclusions
-----------

1. Neither of us had it entirely right. For years git.git's rule
   was the spine you described, and it was Junio's stated
   preference. Since June 2026 it has been the fan, which is what
   the docs and both of its tools now produce.
2. What git.git requires, and Junio enforces, is that every version
   stays in one thread, with each version's patches replying to that
   version's own cover. Which earlier cover a revision replies to is
   a readability choice that the project argued about and settled
   without real consensus.
3. git.git moved to the fan to avoid deep nesting on lore's web view
   and on phones. The spine's advantage, which Weijie argued, is that
   the thread shows which version answers which review, and each
   cover's range-diff sits under the version it is diffed against.

So citing git.git does not settle which shape this list should use.
You read the list in notmuch, not lore on a phone, and our longest
series is ten versions, so the depth problem that drove git.git's
switch hardly applies here. I still recommend the spine. Choose it
as our own rule, and describe it in the docs as git.git's convention
before June 2026 (b4's "send-same-thread = yes"), not as its current
one.

If you agree, v2 of "[PATCH 0/3] Thread each revision under its
first version" [15] replies to v(N-1) and drops the walk to v1. Its
commit messages then describe the spine as this list's rule and cite
git.git's convention before June as its source. rethread.py relinks the list's revisions the same
way. If you prefer to follow git.git's current docs, the posted
series stands as it is, apart from correcting "as git.git does" to
name the June change.

